(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["mth-12-11"] = {"mth-12-11-m01":{"idea":"The definite integral is the change in any antiderivative.","steps":["Find any function g whose derivative is f.","Subtract its value at the lower limit from its value at the upper limit.","The arbitrary constant cancels in the subtraction, so any antiderivative works."],"others":"A function with equal end values would give zero. The second derivative of f goes the wrong way. The derivative of f is a different function.","trap":"Antiderivative, not derivative.","answer":"any antiderivative of f"},"mth-12-11-m02":{"idea":"Fixed limits give one fixed value.","steps":["The limits fix the answer, so no x remains and there is no + c.","The result is a single number.","It may be positive, negative or zero."],"others":"A function of x would remain only for variable limits. A definite integral need not be positive. A family of functions is the indefinite integral.","trap":"Definite means a number.","answer":"a number"},"mth-12-11-m03":{"idea":"Swapping the limits flips the sign.","steps":["The integral from b to a equals the negative of the integral from a to b.","Add the two: they cancel.","The sum is 0."],"others":"The integral from a to b alone would need the other part to be zero. f(b) − f(a) is not the sum. Twice the integral would need equal signs.","trap":"Reverse limits give a minus sign.","answer":"0"},"mth-12-11-m04":{"idea":"Integrate x and put in the limits.","steps":["The antiderivative of x is x²/2.","At 1 it is 1/2 and at 0 it is 0.","The value is 1/2, the area of a right triangle with base 1 and height 1."],"others":"1/3 is the integral of x². 1 would be the area of a square. 2 is too large for this triangle.","trap":"x²/2, not x².","answer":"1/2"},"mth-12-11-m05":{"idea":"Integrate sin x to −cos x.","steps":["The antiderivative of sin x is −cos x.","−cos π − (−cos 0) = 1 + 1.","The value is 2, the area of one arch of the sine curve."],"others":"−2 has the sign wrong, since the arch is above the axis. 0 would be the integral over a full cycle. 1 is the integral over a quarter arch.","trap":"cos π = −1.","answer":"2"},"mth-12-11-m06":{"idea":"Find the antiderivative of 3x².","steps":["The antiderivative of 3x² is x³.","At 1 it is 1 and at 0 it is 0.","The value is 1."],"others":"3 forgets to integrate. 0 would need the same value at both limits. 1/3 is the integral of x² alone.","trap":"The factor 3 is absorbed by the power rule.","answer":"1"},"mth-12-11-m07":{"idea":"The antiderivative of 1/x is log x.","steps":["The antiderivative is log x.","At e, log e = 1. At 1, log 1 = 0.","The value is 1 − 0 = 1."],"others":"e is the upper limit, not the answer. 0 would need equal values at both ends. e − 1 would be the integral of 1.","trap":"log e = 1 and log 1 = 0.","answer":"1"},"mth-12-11-m08":{"idea":"Integrate term by term.","steps":["The antiderivative of 3x² + 1 is x³ + x.","At 2 it is 8 + 2 = 10, and at 0 it is 0.","The value is 10."],"others":"8 forgets the term x. 6 and 14 come from arithmetic slips with the cube.","trap":"Do not forget the integral of the constant 1.","answer":"10"},"mth-12-11-m09":{"idea":"The variable letter is a dummy.","steps":["Writing x or t makes no difference in the value.","The value depends only on the function and the limits.","This is used in the property with a + b − x."],"others":"The value does not depend on the letter. The limits need not be equal. The function need not be constant.","trap":"Dummy variable.","answer":"the name of the variable of integration does not matter"},"mth-12-11-m10":{"idea":"Split the interval where the formula changes.","steps":["Choose a split point c between a and b.","Integrate each formula over its own part.","Add the results. This is needed for |x| at 0 or any piecewise function."],"others":"For an odd function you use symmetry, not splitting. Equal limits give zero directly. A constant function needs no split.","trap":"Split at the point where the rule changes.","answer":"f has a different formula on each part, as |x| does at 0"},"mth-12-11-m11":{"idea":"Replace x by a − x and add the two forms.","steps":["Call the integral I.","Write I again with a − x in place of x.","Add the two forms. The sum often has a simple integrand such as 1, so 2I is easy."],"others":"The property does not differentiate the integral. It does not find an antiderivative without substitution. It does not make the limits infinite.","trap":"Add the original and the reflected form.","answer":"add the integral to its reflected form, so that a hard part cancels"},"mth-12-11-m12":{"idea":"An even function is symmetric about the y-axis.","steps":["The two halves, left and right, have equal area.","So the whole interval from −a to a gives twice the half.","The value is 2 times the integral from 0 to a."],"others":"0 is for odd functions. −2 times has a wrong sign. The single half integral ignores the other half.","trap":"Even: double. Odd: zero.","answer":"2 times the integral of f(x) from 0 to a"},"mth-12-11-m13":{"idea":"An odd function has cancelling halves.","steps":["For odd f, f(−x) = −f(x).","The part below the axis on one side cancels the part above on the other.","So the integral over a symmetric interval is 0."],"others":"A single half is not the answer for odd functions. Twice the half is for even functions. a times f(a) has no meaning here.","trap":"x³ and sin x are odd.","answer":"0"},"mth-12-11-m14":{"idea":"x² is even, so double the half interval.","steps":["x² is even.","The integral from −1 to 1 is 2 times the integral from 0 to 1.","That is 2 × 1/3 = 2/3."],"others":"0 would be right only for an odd function. 2 forgets to integrate. 1/3 is only the half interval.","trap":"Do not treat x² as odd.","answer":"2/3"},"mth-12-11-m15":{"idea":"Use linearity of the integral.","steps":["The integral of 2f(x) is 2 times the integral of f(x), which is 6.","The integral of 1 from 0 to 1 is 1.","Add them: 6 + 1 = 7."],"others":"6 forgets the +1 term. 4 and 5 come from wrong arithmetic with the constant.","trap":"The integral of 1 over a unit interval is 1.","answer":"7"},"mth-12-11-m16":{"idea":"The antiderivative of tan x is log|sec x|.","steps":["Use the antiderivative log|sec x|.","At π/4, sec = √2. At 0, sec = 1.","The value is log √2 − 0 = (1/2) log 2."],"others":"−(1/2) log 2 has a wrong sign. 1/2 forgets the log. log 2 forgets that log √2 is half of log 2.","trap":"log √2 = (1/2) log 2.","answer":"(1/2) log 2"},"mth-12-11-m17":{"idea":"Replace x by π/2 − x and add.","steps":["Let I be the integral. Replace x by π/2 − x.","Then I also equals the integral of cos x/(sin x + cos x).","Adding the two gives 2I = π/2, so I = π/4."],"others":"π/8 forgets to divide by 2 once more than needed. 1/2 is not the length of the interval. π/2 forgets the halving.","trap":"Add the two forms to get the full interval length.","answer":"π/4"},"mth-12-11-m18":{"idea":"Use sin²x = (1 − cos 2x)/2.","steps":["Write sin²x as (1 − cos 2x)/2.","The antiderivative is x/2 − sin 2x/4.","At π: π/2 − 0. At 0: 0. The value is π/2."],"others":"π/4 and π come from slips with the factor 1/2. 1 has no connection to the interval. sin² and cos² share π between them.","trap":"The mean of sin² over a full period is 1/2.","answer":"π/2"},"mth-12-11-m19":{"idea":"Use the reflection property.","steps":["Call the integral I and replace x by a − x.","Then I also equals the integral of f(a − x)/(f(a − x) + f(x)).","Adding the two forms gives 2I = a, so I = a/2."],"others":"0 would need cancelling parts. a is twice the correct value. a²/2 has the wrong units.","trap":"Add the two forms.","answer":"a/2"},"mth-12-11-m20":{"idea":"Integrate by parts with u = x.","steps":["The antiderivative of x sin x is −x cos x + sin x.","At π: π + 0 = π. At 0: 0.","The value is π."],"others":"2 is the integral of sin x. 0 would need equal end values. π/2 forgets the factor from −cos π = 1.","trap":"−cos π = +1.","answer":"π"},"mth-12-11-m21":{"idea":"Use the antiderivative (x − 1)eˣ.","steps":["The antiderivative of x eˣ is (x − 1)eˣ.","At 1 it is 0. At 0 it is −1.","The value is 0 − (−1) = 1."],"others":"0 forgets the lower limit. e − 1 is the integral of eˣ. e is the value of eˣ at 1.","trap":"Subtract the lower value, which is negative.","answer":"1"},"mth-12-11-m22":{"idea":"Split |x| at 0.","steps":["On the interval from −1 to 0, |x| = −x, which gives 1/2.","On the interval from 0 to 2, |x| = x, which gives 2.","The total is 1/2 + 2 = 5/2."],"others":"3 would be x from −1 to 2 without care. 1 forgets the part on [0, 2]. 3/2 treats the left part as negative.","trap":"The area is never negative.","answer":"5/2"},"mth-12-11-m23":{"idea":"Split at a and reflect the second half.","steps":["Split the integral from 0 to 2a at a.","In the second part put x = 2a − t. It becomes the integral of f(2a − t) from 0 to a.","If f(2a − x) = f(x), the two parts are equal, so the total is twice one part."],"others":"If f(2a − x) = −f(x), the parts cancel and the total is 0. An odd function does not give this property by itself. f(a − x) = 0 for all x would make f zero.","trap":"Equal reflection doubles, opposite reflection cancels.","answer":"f(2a − x) = f(x)"},"mth-12-11-m24":{"idea":"Cut the interval into n thin strips.","steps":["Each strip has width h = (b − a)/n.","Each strip's area is about f(a + rh) × h.","Add them and let n grow: the limit of h times the sum of f(a + rh), r from 1 to n."],"others":"Leaving out the factor h makes the sum grow without limit. Starting at f(a) misses the last strip. The other forms leave out the width.","trap":"The width h is essential.","answer":"the limit as n tends to infinity of h × [f(a + h) + f(a + 2h) + … + f(a + nh)], with h = (b − a)/n"},"mth-12-11-m25":{"idea":"Sum first, then take the limit.","steps":["1 + 2 + … + n = n(n + 1)/2.","So the expression is (n + 1)/(2n).","As n grows, this tends to 1/2."],"others":"The limit exists. It is not 1, which would be for (1/n²) times n². It is not 0, since the sum grows like n².","trap":"Leading term of the sum is n²/2.","answer":"1/2"},"mth-12-11-m26":{"idea":"The limits add up to 7, so use the reflection x → 7 − x.","steps":["Here the sum of limits is 2 + 5 = 7.","Replace x by 7 − x to get a second form of the same integral.","Add: 2I = the integral of 1 from 2 to 5 = 3, so I = 3/2."],"others":"3 forgets to halve. 7/2 uses the sum of limits. 5/2 is the upper limit halved.","trap":"Interval length over 2.","answer":"3/2"},"mth-12-11-n01":{"idea":"Integrate and subtract.","steps":["The antiderivative is x² + x.","At 3 it is 12 and at 1 it is 2.","The value is 12 − 2 = 10."],"trap":"Upper minus lower.","answer":"10"},"mth-12-11-n02":{"idea":"Integrate term by term.","steps":["The antiderivative is x⁴/4 − x² + x.","At 2: 4 − 4 + 2 = 2. At 0: 0.","The value is 2."],"trap":"x⁴/4 at 2 is 4.","answer":"2"},"mth-12-11-n03":{"idea":"The antiderivative of sec²x is tan x.","steps":["The antiderivative is tan x.","tan(π/3) − tan 0 = √3 − 0.","√3 = 1.732."],"trap":"tan(π/3) = √3.","answer":"1.732"},"mth-12-11-n04":{"idea":"Substitute t = log x.","steps":["Put t = log x, so dt = dx/x.","The limits become 0 and 1.","The integral of t dt from 0 to 1 is 1/2 = 0.5."],"trap":"Change the limits with the substitution.","answer":"0.5"},"mth-12-11-n05":{"idea":"Integrate by parts.","steps":["The antiderivative is −x cos x + sin x.","At π: π + 0 = π. At 0: 0.","The value is π = 3.142."],"trap":"cos π = −1.","answer":"3.142"},"mth-12-11-n06":{"idea":"Use the reflection x → π/2 − x.","steps":["Let I be the integral. Replace x by π/2 − x.","The new integrand has cos x on top.","Adding, 2I = π/2, so I = π/4 = 0.785."],"trap":"Add the two forms.","answer":"0.785"},"mth-12-11-n07":{"idea":"Split |x| at 0.","steps":["On the interval from −1 to 0, the integral of −x is 1/2.","On the interval from 0 to 2, the integral of x is 2.","The total is 2.5."],"trap":"|x| = −x for negative x.","answer":"2.5"},"mth-12-11-n08":{"idea":"Substitute t = x².","steps":["Put t = x², so x dx = dt/2.","The limits become 0 and 4.","The value is (1/2)(e⁴ − 1) = (54.598 − 1)/2 = 26.799."],"trap":"New limits: 0 and 4.","answer":"26.799"},"mth-12-11-n09":{"idea":"The limits add up to 7, so reflect x → 7 − x.","steps":["Since 2 + 5 = 7, replacing x by 7 − x gives a second form.","Adding the two forms gives 2I = 5 − 2 = 3.","So I = 1.5."],"trap":"Interval length divided by 2.","answer":"1.5"},"mth-12-11-n10":{"idea":"Use the standard tan⁻¹ form.","steps":["The antiderivative is (1/3) tan⁻¹(x/3).","At 3: (1/3)(π/4). At 0: 0.","The value is π/12 = 0.262."],"trap":"tan⁻¹ 1 = π/4.","answer":"0.262"},"mth-12-11-n11":{"idea":"Read the sum as a limit of a sum with h = 1/n.","steps":["With h = 1/n and f(x) = x² on the interval from 0 to 1, the sum is the integral.","The integral of x² from 0 to 1 is 1/3.","Check: the sum is (n + 1)(2n + 1)/(6n²), which tends to 1/3 = 0.333."],"trap":"Recognise the Riemann sum.","answer":"0.333"}};
